    <h1>Where Do Ravens Sleep? Roosts, Cliffs, and Communal Nights</h1>
  </header>

  <section class="qa-snippet" aria-label="Direct answer">
    <p><strong class="claim-science">[science]</strong> Ravens sleep at roost sites — cliffs, tall trees, or large patches of woodland — that may be used by a single pair on their territory or by hundreds of non-breeding birds in communal roosts. Communal roosting is most prominent in non-breeding flocks (young birds and unpaired adults). Paired territory holders roost close to their breeding site year-round.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="qa-section">
    <h2>Two Kinds of Roost</h2>
    <p><strong class="claim-science">[science]</strong> There are two distinct roosting situations in Common Raven populations:<sup><a href="#src-1">1</a></sup></p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Territory roost (paired adults):</strong> once a raven pair has established a breeding territory, they typically roost within or adjacent to it year-round. The roost site is often a tall tree, a cliff ledge, or a rocky outcrop — any elevated, sheltered perch near the core of their territory. The same site may be used for years.</li>
      <li><strong>Communal roost (non-breeders and floaters):</strong> young ravens (1–4 years old) and some unpaired older birds live in non-breeding flocks that use communal roost sites — often patches of dense woodland, cliff systems, or other large, sheltered areas. These communal roosts can be used by dozens to hundreds of birds.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section class="qa-section">
    <h2>Why Roost Communally? The Information-Centre Hypothesis</h2>
    <p><strong class="claim-science">[science]</strong> Bernd Heinrich proposed and tested the "information-centre hypothesis" for raven communal roosting. The idea is that roosting together allows birds that failed to find food on a given day to follow successful foragers to food the following morning. The roost functions as an information exchange — birds that behave like they know where food is (fed, settled behaviour) are followed by birds that don't (hungry, restless).<sup><a href="#src-1">1</a></sup></p>
    <p>Heinrich's field tests in Maine, Vermont, and New Hampshire confirmed key predictions: ravens did follow other ravens from roosts to food sources, and food-deprived birds disproportionately followed fed birds. The hypothesis has been widely accepted for ravens, though some researchers note that anti-predator benefits and passive warmth sharing may also contribute to communal roosting.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="qa-section">
    <h2>Roost Site Characteristics</h2>
    <p><strong class="claim-science">[science]</strong> Raven roost sites tend to share certain features:<sup><a href="#src-1">1</a></sup><sup><a href="#src-2">2</a></sup></p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Elevation:</strong> tall trees, cliffs, and ridgelines are preferred — elevated perches give ravens early warning of approaching predators and may offer wind shelter.</li>
      <li><strong>Consistency:</strong> the same sites are used repeatedly, sometimes for many years. Local people near raven roosts often notice the regular flight of birds to the same woodland at dusk.</li>
      <li><strong>Winter concentration:</strong> roosts grow largest in autumn and winter when young birds from the year's breeding season have dispersed and food is concentrated (e.g., at winter carcasses, abattoir waste).</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

